1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,750


2
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:22,060
ADAM MARTIN: So, last semester,
my grandfather passed away,

3
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:28,000
and I was responsible for
explaining to my two sons

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,210
how a funeral works.

5
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:32,619
So I'm a professor, right?

6
00:00:32,619 --> 00:00:36,220
I pride myself on being able
to explain things clearly.

7
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,880
So I went to tell
my five-year-old son

8
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:42,220
sort of what's going
on during the funeral.

9
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:47,375
I told him, your papa's body is
up in that wooden box up there.

10
00:00:47,375 --> 00:00:49,000
We're going to
celebrate him right now.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,070
We're going to go
to the cemetery,

12
00:00:51,070 --> 00:00:53,760
and then we're
going to bury him.

13
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:55,960
And you know, I'm a professor.

14
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,390
I thought I nailed it, OK?

15
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:01,000
Except my son was
showing this looking

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,890
look of concern in his eyes.

17
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:03,790
And he looked at me.

18
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:06,940
He was like, what
about his head?

19
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:11,320
So my plea for this
semester is please let me

20
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,815
know when I've forgotten
the part about the head, OK?

21
00:01:14,815 --> 00:01:16,440
You know, you guys
are listening to me.

22
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,160
I might have forgotten
something that,

23
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:21,670
to me, seems kind of obvious,
but that, for you, it

24
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:25,600
would help to know to
understand the material.

25
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,930
So today, we're going
to start with cells.

26
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:33,190
And so here, you can see this
is one of my favorite movies.

27
00:01:33,190 --> 00:01:37,000
This is a neutrophil cell
here that's migrating.

28
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,010
You can see it's chasing
after this smaller

29
00:01:39,010 --> 00:01:42,040
cell which is a bacteria cell.

30
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:45,125
And around this neutrophil,
what you're seeing here

31
00:01:45,125 --> 00:01:46,750
are these red blood
cells, which aren't

32
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:50,380
doing very much of anything.

33
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,990
So the point I'm
making with this video

34
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:58,330
is that cells have a
huge amount of diversity.

35
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,100
So cells have diversity.

36
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,930


37
00:02:04,930 --> 00:02:10,460
As you can see from the video,
there's a diversity in size.

38
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:15,035
There's diversity in shape and
also diversity in behavior.

39
00:02:15,035 --> 00:02:22,970


40
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:25,070
OK, so we're going to
unpack this a little bit.

41
00:02:25,070 --> 00:02:29,110
And I just want to start
by just pointing out

42
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:32,050
that until now in the
semester, we've dealt mainly

43
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:37,390
with very small things, such as
atoms, small molecules, lipids,

44
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:38,650
and proteins.

45
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:41,410
And the size of these
structures, they're

46
00:02:41,410 --> 00:02:43,870
on the nanometer scale.

47
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:46,585
Now, cells are a
unit up in size.

48
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:49,390


49
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:54,520
Cells span several orders
of magnitude in size.

50
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,770
So bacterial cells
are on the small side.

51
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:00,970
They're on the 1 micron
to 10 micron size.

52
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:02,290
So you can see that here.

53
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:06,340
Most bacteria is about
1 micron to 10 micron.

54
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:11,170
But our cells span from tens of
microns to hundreds of microns,

55
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:15,490
and even larger than that,
because the human egg

56
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:18,120
is on the order of a
millimeter in size.

57
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:20,120
This is not a human egg,
but this is a frog egg.

58
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:23,560
You can see often the egg cells
are the biggest of all cells.

59
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,470
The biggest cell
is an ostrich egg.

60
00:03:26,470 --> 00:03:29,440
It's about 15
centimeters, I believe.

61
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:35,238
So cells span a huge,
wide range of sizes.

62
00:03:35,238 --> 00:03:37,030
I'm going to start with
the simplest, which

63
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:38,770
is a bacteria cell.

64
00:03:38,770 --> 00:03:41,230
And what I want to point
out about this cell

65
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,150
is its simplicity.

66
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,990
So here, you can see this
is an electron micrograph

67
00:03:46,990 --> 00:03:49,160
of bacterial cell here.

68
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,160
And this is a cartoon
just illustrating

69
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:52,900
some of the key features.

70
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:56,540
You have a plasma
membrane and a cell wall.

71
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:00,750
The cell wall here is in sort
of-- the periplasmic space

72
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:02,320
is in green.

73
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:05,440
And this encapsulates
the cytoplasm.

74
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,690
And the only other real
structure you can see,

75
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:12,440
in this case, is this nucleoid
structure in the middle.

76
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:13,975
And what the
nucleoid is, is it's

77
00:04:13,975 --> 00:04:16,300
just the chromosome
of the bacterium.

78
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:19,120


79
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,700
And I want to point out
that later on in the course,

80
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:26,850
Professor Imperiali is going
to come back and tell you

81
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:29,890
about antibiotics and
how bacteria can develop

82
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:33,580
antibiotic resistance, which
is of critical importance

83
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:38,080
for biology and medicine.

84
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:41,280
So our cells are more
complicated than this.

85
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:43,920
And that's because if
you look at this EM here,

86
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:45,930
you can see that
eukaryotic cells--

87
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:47,900
and we are eukaryotes--

88
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,840
have membrane-bound
compartments.

89
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,900
There's a nucleus here that
houses our nuclear DNA.

90
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:59,190
And also, there's a series
of membrane compartments

91
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:00,870
that span the cytoplasm.

92
00:05:00,870 --> 00:05:03,570


93
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:08,880
Now, our cells, even in a single
organism, such as us, our cells

94
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:15,620
have incredible diversity
and specialization.

95
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:19,220
So there's diversity
within a single organism.

96
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,400
There is specialization.

97
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:25,410


98
00:05:25,410 --> 00:05:30,110
So as we develop
from a single cell,

99
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,720
our cells acquire
properties that

100
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:38,640
allow them to carry out specific
functions in our bodies.

101
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:41,540
And an extreme example
of this is shown up here.

102
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:46,760
These are pictures or drawings
of neurons from Ramon y Cajal,

103
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:48,740
and you can see how
this looks nothing

104
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:52,640
like the cartoon picture of
a cell I just showed you.

105
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:56,030
These cells have
highly dendritic sort

106
00:05:56,030 --> 00:05:58,010
of arrays of protrusions.

107
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:01,160
And these cells
have evolved such

108
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:04,940
that they are very good at sort
of transmitting information

109
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:06,140
in the body, right?

110
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:09,260
An extreme example of a
nerve is a sciatic nerve,

111
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:12,020
which extends from the
base of your spinal cord

112
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:13,650
all the way down into your foot.

113
00:06:13,650 --> 00:06:16,010
So it's about a meter long.

114
00:06:16,010 --> 00:06:20,120
That's an extreme
specialization for a cell.

115
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:22,490
So these cells are
specialized, but what's

116
00:06:22,490 --> 00:06:28,190
important to note is that
within a single organism,

117
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:33,530
the genomic DNA of a cell
is more or less the same.

118
00:06:33,530 --> 00:06:42,140
So genomic DNA is the same, with
some exceptions, some of which

119
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:43,700
we'll get to in the course.

120
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:45,930
The genomic DNA is the same.

121
00:06:45,930 --> 00:06:47,750
What's different
is the genes that

122
00:06:47,750 --> 00:06:51,740
are being expressed in
these cells and the proteins

123
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:54,680
that they encode that give
these cells different functions.

124
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,260


125
00:06:57,260 --> 00:07:01,910
So what's different and
what allows cells to acquire

126
00:07:01,910 --> 00:07:04,970
these different functionalities
is this different gene

127
00:07:04,970 --> 00:07:06,085
expression.

128
00:07:06,085 --> 00:07:21,140


129
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:23,310
OK, so that's the overview.

130
00:07:23,310 --> 00:07:25,340
Now I want to talk
about compartments.

131
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,100


132
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:32,690
And if we go back
to this cartoon

133
00:07:32,690 --> 00:07:34,612
that I showed you from your--

134
00:07:34,612 --> 00:07:37,130
oh, I just wanted to
point out that right now,

135
00:07:37,130 --> 00:07:41,180
at MIT and the Broad,
there is a project

136
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:46,490
that's ongoing to really
define all the cell types that

137
00:07:46,490 --> 00:07:48,750
are present in humans.

138
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:52,970
And this is known as
the Human Cell Atlas.

139
00:07:52,970 --> 00:07:54,470
And I just want you
to take a minute

140
00:07:54,470 --> 00:07:59,180
to think about if you wanted
to define different cell types,

141
00:07:59,180 --> 00:08:03,110
what would you look at to
classify these cell types?

142
00:08:03,110 --> 00:08:05,150
Anyone have an idea?

143
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:05,650
Yes?

144
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:06,160
What's your name?

145
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:07,020
AUDIENCE: Rachel.

146
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:07,900
ADAM MARTIN: Rachel.

147
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:09,310
AUDIENCE: Cell function.

148
00:08:09,310 --> 00:08:09,640
ADAM MARTIN: What's that?

149
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:10,450
AUDIENCE: Function.

150
00:08:10,450 --> 00:08:12,970
ADAM MARTIN: You could
look at function.

151
00:08:12,970 --> 00:08:16,960
That might be a little
subjective in how to interpret.

152
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,191
What defines the
function of the cell?

153
00:08:19,191 --> 00:08:20,995
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking
like what it does

154
00:08:20,995 --> 00:08:22,790
and how it works
with other cells.

155
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:24,290
ADAM MARTIN: But
is there something,

156
00:08:24,290 --> 00:08:25,850
I guess, within
that cell that would

157
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:29,010
define what its function is?

158
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:29,510
Yeah?

159
00:08:29,510 --> 00:08:30,450
What's your name?

160
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:31,213
AUDIENCE: Samantar

161
00:08:31,213 --> 00:08:32,130
ADAM MARTIN: Samantar?

162
00:08:32,130 --> 00:08:33,200
AUDIENCE: Samantar

163
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:35,150
ADAM MARTIN: Samantac
All right, yeah?

164
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:36,530
AUDIENCE: Gene expression data.

165
00:08:36,530 --> 00:08:38,030
ADAM MARTIN: Gene
expression, right?

166
00:08:38,030 --> 00:08:41,570
What genes are these
cells expressing?

167
00:08:41,570 --> 00:08:45,680
So what they're doing is
they're isolating single cells

168
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:47,790
from tissues.

169
00:08:47,790 --> 00:08:49,920
And then they're looking
at gene expression.

170
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,820
So which type of
molecule here would

171
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:55,640
be the molecule you'd want
to look at if you want

172
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:57,172
to look at gene expression?

173
00:08:57,172 --> 00:09:04,070


174
00:09:04,070 --> 00:09:05,880
Not Miles.

175
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:07,182
Malik.

176
00:09:07,182 --> 00:09:09,265
AUDIENCE: You look at mRNA.

177
00:09:09,265 --> 00:09:10,390
ADAM MARTIN: mRNA, exactly.

178
00:09:10,390 --> 00:09:12,350
Malik is exactly right.

179
00:09:12,350 --> 00:09:14,330
So you look at the mRNA, right?

180
00:09:14,330 --> 00:09:19,550
Because if there's mRNA, that
means that gene was expressed,

181
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:24,452
and it's possibly encoding
the translation of a protein.

182
00:09:24,452 --> 00:09:25,660
So that's what they're doing.

183
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:29,450
They're isolating single cells
and doing a massive single cell

184
00:09:29,450 --> 00:09:31,100
RNA seek project.

185
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:33,140
And that's helping
to identify new cell

186
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:36,010
types in the human body.

187
00:09:36,010 --> 00:09:38,930
OK, coming back to compartments.

188
00:09:38,930 --> 00:09:40,550
So I showed you this picture.

189
00:09:40,550 --> 00:09:44,420
You can see there are lots of
membrane-bound compartments

190
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:45,040
in the cell.

191
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:46,880
You can see the
lines in this cartoon

192
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:49,040
represent lipid bilayers.

193
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,410
And so these are
compartments that

194
00:09:51,410 --> 00:09:56,373
are completely encapsulated
by a lipid bilayer.

195
00:09:56,373 --> 00:09:58,040
And I'm going to
remind you of something

196
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:01,100
that Professor Imperiali
told you about already.

197
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:02,720
And the reason I'm
saying it again

198
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,320
is because it is so
freaking important, OK?

199
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:10,670
So if we consider
a lipid bilayer

200
00:10:10,670 --> 00:10:14,330
where the circles are the
polar head groups of the lipids

201
00:10:14,330 --> 00:10:17,900
and the squiggly lines are
the fatty acid chains--

202
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,135
I'm not going to draw all
the fatty acid chains.

203
00:10:20,135 --> 00:10:24,635


204
00:10:24,635 --> 00:10:25,760
So this is a lipid bilayer.

205
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:31,460


206
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:37,810
And these head groups, are they
hydrophilic or hydrophobic?

207
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:38,310
Yes?

208
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:38,760
What's your name?

209
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:39,140
AUDIENCE: Stephen.

210
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:40,184
ADAM MARTIN: Stephen.

211
00:10:40,184 --> 00:10:40,940
AUDIENCE: They're hydrophilic.

212
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:41,880
ADAM MARTIN:
They're hydrophilic.

213
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:42,840
They like water, right?

214
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:46,200
The water is on the
different faces here.

215
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:47,610
These are hydrophilic.

216
00:10:47,610 --> 00:10:53,680


217
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:56,570
How about this
central region here?

218
00:10:56,570 --> 00:10:57,070
Yes?

219
00:10:57,070 --> 00:10:57,570
Name?

220
00:10:57,570 --> 00:10:58,240
AUDIENCE: Ory.

221
00:10:58,240 --> 00:10:59,003
ADAM MARTIN: Ory.

222
00:10:59,003 --> 00:10:59,920
AUDIENCE: Hydrophobic.

223
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:02,620
ADAM MARTIN:
Hydrophobic, good, right?

224
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:05,230
That's excellent.

225
00:11:05,230 --> 00:11:08,910
So these are hydrophobic.

226
00:11:08,910 --> 00:11:10,360
Hydrophobic.

227
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:14,080
So what that means is you have
this hydrophobic layer that is

228
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:16,600
surrounding each of
these compartments,

229
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:20,050
and that's going to serve
as a barrier such that water

230
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:23,050
and things dissolved in water
cannot pass through this

231
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:27,580
barrier unless
something allows it to.

232
00:11:27,580 --> 00:11:30,730
So each of these compartments
are membrane bound,

233
00:11:30,730 --> 00:11:34,750
have properties in
the lumen that's

234
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:36,490
in the interior of
the compartment that's

235
00:11:36,490 --> 00:11:39,400
going to be different from
that of the cytoplasm.

236
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:42,310
And the cell, the
interior of the cytoplasm,

237
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:45,310
is going to be different from
that of the extracellular

238
00:11:45,310 --> 00:11:47,560
space.

239
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:49,750
So let me just give
you some examples

240
00:11:49,750 --> 00:11:53,620
of how things are different
inside and outside the cell

241
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,320
and also inside and
outside these compartments.

242
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:07,510
So if we consider the
plasma versus the cytoplasm,

243
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:11,720
we can consider the
concentration of various ions.

244
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:14,080
And I want to get the
concentrations right.

245
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:21,430
Let's consider sodium, which is
a monovalent cation, potassium,

246
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:22,960
and calcium.

247
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:26,290
I'm just going to use
these as illustrations.

248
00:12:26,290 --> 00:12:30,190
So sodium is concentrated
outside the cell,

249
00:12:30,190 --> 00:12:34,990
about 150 millimolar,
and is less concentrated

250
00:12:34,990 --> 00:12:36,910
in the cytoplasm.

251
00:12:36,910 --> 00:12:41,800
So there's a huge difference
in sodium between the outside

252
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:44,740
and the inside of the cell.

253
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:47,320
Potassium-- now, you
might think potassium

254
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:48,760
would be a lot like sodium.

255
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:50,830
It's a monovalent cation.

256
00:12:50,830 --> 00:12:52,660
It's a similar size.

257
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:55,350
But it actually shows
the flip distribution.

258
00:12:55,350 --> 00:12:59,590
So it's four millimolar
in the exoplasm

259
00:12:59,590 --> 00:13:05,350
and 140 millimolar
in the cytoplasm, OK?

260
00:13:05,350 --> 00:13:08,440
So there appears to be some
selectivity here, right?

261
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:12,070
The cell is concentrating
certain things

262
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:15,850
inside the cytoplasm, and
it's excluding others.

263
00:13:15,850 --> 00:13:24,010
So there's selectivity, even
between closely related atoms

264
00:13:24,010 --> 00:13:25,480
here.

265
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:31,450
OK, calcium is two millimolar
in the exoplasm and around 10

266
00:13:31,450 --> 00:13:35,200
to the negative fifth
millimolar in the cytoplasm.

267
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:38,290


268
00:13:38,290 --> 00:13:42,370
So because there are these huge
gradients in the concentration

269
00:13:42,370 --> 00:13:45,580
of these ions leads
you to expect that this

270
00:13:45,580 --> 00:13:48,040
is a non-equilibrium state.

271
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:51,283
Because if it were equilibrium,
these ions would go in and out,

272
00:13:51,283 --> 00:13:53,950
and they'd equilibrate such that
the same concentration would be

273
00:13:53,950 --> 00:13:57,160
on the inside as the outside.

274
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:58,840
And so there's selectivity.

275
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,950
And also, there's
non-equilibrium,

276
00:14:00,950 --> 00:14:03,880
which suggests that
energy is required

277
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:06,460
to maintain this asymmetry.

278
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:10,380


279
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:12,890
I want to point out it's
not just the concentrations

280
00:14:12,890 --> 00:14:16,010
of various molecules that are
different between the inside

281
00:14:16,010 --> 00:14:19,640
and the outside, but the
plasma membrane can also

282
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:21,140
have a voltage across it.

283
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:25,310


284
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:27,890
So this is exoplasm.

285
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:29,060
This is cytoplasm.

286
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:32,580


287
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:35,915
And this membrane
can hold a voltage.

288
00:14:35,915 --> 00:14:40,740


289
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:43,080
And this is going to become
incredibly important when

290
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:48,870
we talk about neurons,
because neurons use changes

291
00:14:48,870 --> 00:14:54,000
in this voltage to transmit
signals across their length

292
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,450
and also to transmit
signals at the synapse.

293
00:14:57,450 --> 00:14:59,920
And we'll talk about
that later in the course.

294
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:02,540
So this is known as
membrane potential,

295
00:15:02,540 --> 00:15:04,596
this voltage difference.

296
00:15:04,596 --> 00:15:15,270


297
00:15:15,270 --> 00:15:20,520
I also, again, want to point
out this endomembrane system

298
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:24,150
in here, where the
gray regions here

299
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:26,400
are sort of compartments
that will communicate

300
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:28,540
with each other.

301
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:31,050
And so there's this
whole internal structure

302
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:34,950
of endomembrane system
which is compartmentalized

303
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:38,610
from the cytoplasm, OK?

304
00:15:38,610 --> 00:15:41,130
And so now I want to talk
about how do things get

305
00:15:41,130 --> 00:15:44,280
in and out of this structure.

306
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:47,880
So I think we're up to
three, getting in and out.

307
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:56,280


308
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:59,520
So cells-- and this is
very important in cell

309
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:01,400
communication, right?

310
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:05,160
For cells to
communicate, cells have

311
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:07,920
to send things, like
signals, to other cells.

312
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:11,220
And also, cells
can take in stuff

313
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:15,220
and sort of receive
things from other cells.

314
00:16:15,220 --> 00:16:18,870
So how is it that this happens?

315
00:16:18,870 --> 00:16:22,500
Well, if we consider the
plasma membrane of a cell--

316
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:25,080
this is a lipid bilayer, PM.

317
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:28,410
I abbreviate Plasma
Membrane, PM.

318
00:16:28,410 --> 00:16:30,200
So that's a lipid bilayer.

319
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:37,320
And let's say that there's
some type of sort of molecule

320
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:39,700
that's on the
outside of the cell.

321
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:44,400
So, in this case, this
would be exoplasm out here.

322
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:48,930
This would be inside
the cytoplasm here.

323
00:16:48,930 --> 00:16:51,900
Let's say this cell
wanted to sort of take up

324
00:16:51,900 --> 00:16:57,810
this pink structure
into the cell.

325
00:16:57,810 --> 00:16:59,380
How would it do that?

326
00:16:59,380 --> 00:17:02,670
Well, this pink structure,
because it's hydrophilic,

327
00:17:02,670 --> 00:17:06,990
cannot pass through
the lipid bilayer.

328
00:17:06,990 --> 00:17:09,390
So the cell has to
use another strategy

329
00:17:09,390 --> 00:17:11,109
to take this up into the cell.

330
00:17:11,109 --> 00:17:17,089


331
00:17:17,089 --> 00:17:17,760
Let's see.

332
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:19,540
I'll use a little blue here.

333
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:23,319
So let's say the region of the
membrane right here is in blue.

334
00:17:23,319 --> 00:17:31,850
Then, what can happen is this
blue structure can invaginate,

335
00:17:31,850 --> 00:17:34,370
and it can take up
this pink molecule.

336
00:17:34,370 --> 00:17:37,160


337
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:41,360
So the plasma membrane
can invaginate.

338
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:44,030
The plasma membrane
can invaginate,

339
00:17:44,030 --> 00:17:46,700
and if it has this
pink molecule,

340
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:50,960
then if there is a
cision event here,

341
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:54,260
you've now gone from
having your pink molecule

342
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:56,390
on the outside of
the cell to having

343
00:17:56,390 --> 00:18:02,090
the pink molecule in this
vesicle or circular structure

344
00:18:02,090 --> 00:18:04,640
on the inside of the cell.

345
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:07,880
So here now we have this.

346
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:10,730
We have our vesicle in blue.

347
00:18:10,730 --> 00:18:15,140
And now the cell has picked
up this pink molecule,

348
00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:17,900
such like this.

349
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:22,940
So this process of
sort of taking material

350
00:18:22,940 --> 00:18:25,010
from the outside and
sort of bringing it

351
00:18:25,010 --> 00:18:27,500
into the inside of
the cell is known

352
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:38,180
as endocytosis, which is the
process of taking something

353
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:42,770
from the outside and bringing
it in the cell, this is also

354
00:18:42,770 --> 00:18:44,840
a way that viruses
can get in your cells,

355
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:49,010
which is a more nefarious
way that this system is used.

356
00:18:49,010 --> 00:18:51,530
All right, I need a volunteer.

357
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:52,740
Yes.

358
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:54,290
Ory, come on down.

359
00:18:54,290 --> 00:18:59,510


360
00:18:59,510 --> 00:19:01,490
Don't worry.

361
00:19:01,490 --> 00:19:03,120
This'll be simple.

362
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:06,740
I just need you to put
pressure on my head right here.

363
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:07,940
So you see the tassel.

364
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:09,740
Make sure they can
see the tassel.

365
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:11,130
You see this tassel?

366
00:19:11,130 --> 00:19:13,220
This tassel is
that pink molecule,

367
00:19:13,220 --> 00:19:16,640
is right now on
the outside, right?

368
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:19,750
But I'm going to endocytose
it by going like that, right?

369
00:19:19,750 --> 00:19:20,990
And I also got a hand.

370
00:19:20,990 --> 00:19:22,250
That's great.

371
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:25,670
So you see, that's
basically endocytosis.

372
00:19:25,670 --> 00:19:29,630
I just endocytosed my tassel.

373
00:19:29,630 --> 00:19:30,980
All right, you can go up.

374
00:19:30,980 --> 00:19:32,420
We're all set, yeah.

375
00:19:32,420 --> 00:19:35,690
I can do the next one.

376
00:19:35,690 --> 00:19:38,630
OK, so the opposite
of this process--

377
00:19:38,630 --> 00:19:39,880
let me get another color here.

378
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:44,360


379
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:47,930
You can also have vesicles that
are on the inside that then

380
00:19:47,930 --> 00:19:51,590
fuse with the plasma membrane
and release their contents

381
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:52,280
to the exterior.

382
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:56,360


383
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:58,710
And this is called exocytosis.

384
00:19:58,710 --> 00:20:02,900


385
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:05,930
So exocytosis is
something that's

386
00:20:05,930 --> 00:20:11,690
starting out inside one of these
vesicles and then goes out, OK?

387
00:20:11,690 --> 00:20:13,850
So this is not
exactly reversible,

388
00:20:13,850 --> 00:20:16,400
because there are different
protein machineries that

389
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:20,060
mediate either
endocytosis or exocytosis.

390
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:22,385
So now I'm going to
exocytose my tassel.

391
00:20:22,385 --> 00:20:24,920


392
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:25,730
There we go.

393
00:20:25,730 --> 00:20:29,210
So I just exocytosed it, and
now my molecules is again

394
00:20:29,210 --> 00:20:32,930
facing the outside world.

395
00:20:32,930 --> 00:20:36,320
So this is a way for cells
to sort of taken things

396
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:41,630
and also to secrete molecules,
like signaling molecules,

397
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:43,430
into the extracellular space.

398
00:20:43,430 --> 00:20:47,220


399
00:20:47,220 --> 00:20:50,090
So now, we're moving
on, and now we're

400
00:20:50,090 --> 00:20:54,980
going to talk about compartments
within compartments.

401
00:20:54,980 --> 00:20:57,050
So we're on four--

402
00:20:57,050 --> 00:21:04,370
compartments within
compartments.

403
00:21:04,370 --> 00:21:07,550
And you're going to see how
this relates to endocytosis

404
00:21:07,550 --> 00:21:08,400
in just a minute.

405
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:11,670


406
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:16,100
So compartments
within compartments.

407
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:18,350
So the example I'm
going to use here

408
00:21:18,350 --> 00:21:21,020
is an organelle that
is present in us,

409
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:23,690
in animal cells, which
is the mitochondria.

410
00:21:23,690 --> 00:21:31,970


411
00:21:31,970 --> 00:21:35,240
And you all know
that the mitochondria

412
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:39,530
is the powerhouse of the cell.

413
00:21:39,530 --> 00:21:42,230
Whenever I say mitochondria is
the powerhouse of the cells,

414
00:21:42,230 --> 00:21:46,155
I have to do 10 push-ups,
because it's so cliche.

415
00:21:46,155 --> 00:21:52,580


416
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:54,800
I mean, my five-year-old
knows that mitochondria

417
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:56,580
is the powerhouse of the cell.

418
00:21:56,580 --> 00:22:00,200
It's a gross
oversimplification, OK?

419
00:22:00,200 --> 00:22:02,810
Mitochondria are way more
interesting than that,

420
00:22:02,810 --> 00:22:05,630
and I'll show you
in just a minute.

421
00:22:05,630 --> 00:22:10,160
All right, I'll
draw mitochondria,

422
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:13,910
the same mitochondria
that my kids draw me.

423
00:22:13,910 --> 00:22:16,220
So here's a mitochondria.

424
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:18,950
I'm drawing first
the outer membrane.

425
00:22:18,950 --> 00:22:24,430


426
00:22:24,430 --> 00:22:27,430
So there's an outer membrane
to the mitochondria.

427
00:22:27,430 --> 00:22:29,785
And this organelle also
has an inner membrane.

428
00:22:29,785 --> 00:22:34,145


429
00:22:34,145 --> 00:22:35,395
So this is the inner membrane.

430
00:22:35,395 --> 00:22:43,970


431
00:22:43,970 --> 00:22:46,735
The inside here is
called the matrix.

432
00:22:46,735 --> 00:22:49,990


433
00:22:49,990 --> 00:22:54,220
I'll draw some DNA
molecules there.

434
00:22:54,220 --> 00:22:59,650
So this is known as the
mitochondrial DNA, which

435
00:22:59,650 --> 00:23:00,910
I'll mention in just a minute.

436
00:23:00,910 --> 00:23:05,970


437
00:23:05,970 --> 00:23:08,550
So this is the mitochondria.

438
00:23:08,550 --> 00:23:12,420
Where could such an organelle
come from, evolutionarily

439
00:23:12,420 --> 00:23:14,330
speaking?

440
00:23:14,330 --> 00:23:18,090
You know, one problem that
eukaryotic cells have is they

441
00:23:18,090 --> 00:23:21,390
cannot make
mitochondria de novo.

442
00:23:21,390 --> 00:23:24,360
The way that
mitochondria is passed on

443
00:23:24,360 --> 00:23:27,240
is it's replicated
during cell division,

444
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:30,540
and it's passed on from
one cell to the next.

445
00:23:30,540 --> 00:23:33,420
And you guys all got
your mitochondrial DNA

446
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:38,220
and your mitochondria, at least
initially, from your mother.

447
00:23:38,220 --> 00:23:42,180
So this is not an organelle
that can be synthesized de novo.

448
00:23:42,180 --> 00:23:44,220
And the fact that
this is the case

449
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:48,030
has led to a theory known
as the endosymbiont theory,

450
00:23:48,030 --> 00:23:52,830
or hypothesis, which basically
states that there was

451
00:23:52,830 --> 00:23:55,650
an ancestral eukaryotic cell.

452
00:23:55,650 --> 00:23:59,490
And the way that organelles such
as mitochondria and plastids

453
00:23:59,490 --> 00:24:04,770
were derived is from
engulfing bacterial cells that

454
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:07,440
were either capable of
oxidative phosphorylation,

455
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:13,200
in the case of mitochondria, or
were capable of photosynthesis,

456
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:17,090
in the case of chloroplasts.

457
00:24:17,090 --> 00:24:21,630
And so this engulfment is much
like this endocytic process

458
00:24:21,630 --> 00:24:22,980
that I just talked to you.

459
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:25,710
It's not really endocytosis,
because these bacteria

460
00:24:25,710 --> 00:24:28,590
are much better than
an endocytic vesicle.

461
00:24:28,590 --> 00:24:34,800
So it's more of like a
macro sort of pinocytosis

462
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:36,480
or something like that, OK?

463
00:24:36,480 --> 00:24:40,470


464
00:24:40,470 --> 00:24:45,600
So something you may have
seen in the news lately--

465
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:50,280
so some of the evidence for
this endosymbiont theory

466
00:24:50,280 --> 00:24:54,240
is that mitochondria and
plastids have their own DNA,

467
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:56,520
OK?

468
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:59,310
So these organelles
have retained DNA.

469
00:24:59,310 --> 00:25:06,660


470
00:25:06,660 --> 00:25:10,110
The genes in the DNA
encode for proteins, that

471
00:25:10,110 --> 00:25:12,280
function in the mitochondria.

472
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:16,630
It also includes ribosomal
RNAs and transfer RNAs

473
00:25:16,630 --> 00:25:19,110
that are required
for protein synthesis

474
00:25:19,110 --> 00:25:21,270
within the mitochondria.

475
00:25:21,270 --> 00:25:24,390
But a lot of the genes that
are required for a functioning

476
00:25:24,390 --> 00:25:28,440
organism have now been
exported to our nuclear DNA,

477
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:30,630
and those genes are made
and proteins are produced,

478
00:25:30,630 --> 00:25:33,120
and then they're imported
into the mitochondria.

479
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:37,620
But the mitochondria has
retained a number of genes,

480
00:25:37,620 --> 00:25:41,490
and it's encoded in
the mitochondrial DNA.

481
00:25:41,490 --> 00:25:43,050
Another reason to
think that this

482
00:25:43,050 --> 00:25:46,500
could be from a symbiotic
relationship, this ancient,

483
00:25:46,500 --> 00:25:49,410
between eukaryotic
and prokaryotic cells

484
00:25:49,410 --> 00:25:53,070
is that these organelles
divide by fission

485
00:25:53,070 --> 00:25:56,700
similar to how bacteria divide.

486
00:25:56,700 --> 00:25:58,110
So they divide by fission.

487
00:25:58,110 --> 00:26:06,020


488
00:26:06,020 --> 00:26:08,270
So I'll show you a
real mitochondria

489
00:26:08,270 --> 00:26:11,420
and a mitochondria undergoing
fission in just a minute.

490
00:26:11,420 --> 00:26:15,950
I just want to point out
that in the news recently,

491
00:26:15,950 --> 00:26:20,480
there's been talk of
three-parent babies.

492
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:24,300
And I just want to explain
to you what that is.

493
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:27,590
So eggs that are--

494
00:26:27,590 --> 00:26:31,580
I shouldn't say
embryos, but eggs

495
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:35,360
that have the nuclear
DNA from two parents

496
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:39,380
can then be given mitochondrial
DNA from another parent.

497
00:26:39,380 --> 00:26:41,900
So these three-parent
babies are essentially

498
00:26:41,900 --> 00:26:45,350
babies that have
nuclear DNA from two

499
00:26:45,350 --> 00:26:50,900
parents but mitochondrial
DNA from a third parent.

500
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:55,010
So here's just an article in
the NewScientist reporting

501
00:26:55,010 --> 00:26:59,450
that this is imminent,
and now it's been done.

502
00:26:59,450 --> 00:27:01,520
Now, why would you
might want to do that?

503
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:03,920
Anyone know why someone
would want to do this?

504
00:27:03,920 --> 00:27:07,470


505
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:08,296
Yes?

506
00:27:08,296 --> 00:27:10,713
AUDIENCE: There might be a
defect in the mitochondrial DNA

507
00:27:10,713 --> 00:27:11,950
of both parents.

508
00:27:11,950 --> 00:27:12,700
ADAM MARTIN: Yeah.

509
00:27:12,700 --> 00:27:13,747
So Stephen, right?

510
00:27:13,747 --> 00:27:14,330
AUDIENCE: Yes.

511
00:27:14,330 --> 00:27:16,560
ADAM MARTIN: Stephen's
exactly right, right?

512
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:19,020
There are diseases
that are so associated

513
00:27:19,020 --> 00:27:22,150
with faulty mitochondrial DNA.

514
00:27:22,150 --> 00:27:26,970
And if you're a mother and
you have the mutations that

515
00:27:26,970 --> 00:27:30,510
cause this disease, this
would be a way for you

516
00:27:30,510 --> 00:27:35,550
to have a child without passing
on the disease to your child.

517
00:27:35,550 --> 00:27:39,690
And so this is something
that is still controversial,

518
00:27:39,690 --> 00:27:43,680
but that's why people are
exploring the opportunity

519
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:48,460
for making these so-called
three-parent babies.

520
00:27:48,460 --> 00:27:51,120
Again, I've a pet peeve
with textbook pictures

521
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:52,590
of mitochondria.

522
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:54,150
Here's your textbook picture.

523
00:27:54,150 --> 00:27:55,950
Everything's labeled nicely.

524
00:27:55,950 --> 00:27:59,220
This is what mitochondria
look like in real life.

525
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:02,670
Mitochondria, like the
endoplasmic reticulum,

526
00:28:02,670 --> 00:28:06,360
actually, form these tubular
networks that essentially

527
00:28:06,360 --> 00:28:08,790
span the entire cell.

528
00:28:08,790 --> 00:28:11,460
So it's convenient for
us to depict mitochondria

529
00:28:11,460 --> 00:28:14,430
like this in textbook,
but mitochondria are

530
00:28:14,430 --> 00:28:16,140
way more interesting than that.

531
00:28:16,140 --> 00:28:18,240
They're dynamic organelles.

532
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:21,030
And I also want to make
the point-- we kind of talk

533
00:28:21,030 --> 00:28:23,040
about these organelles
like they behave

534
00:28:23,040 --> 00:28:25,980
as these separate
entities, but in fact, they

535
00:28:25,980 --> 00:28:27,250
interact with each other.

536
00:28:27,250 --> 00:28:30,810
And there's lots of interesting
biology behind that.

537
00:28:30,810 --> 00:28:33,210
So I'm going to show
you one movie that's

538
00:28:33,210 --> 00:28:37,350
from work done by Gia Voeltz,
and this Friedman person

539
00:28:37,350 --> 00:28:38,920
is the first author.

540
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:41,100
She just gave a talk
here at MIT and showed

541
00:28:41,100 --> 00:28:43,030
some beautiful movies.

542
00:28:43,030 --> 00:28:44,970
All right, focus
on this movie here.

543
00:28:44,970 --> 00:28:49,350
The ER is labeled in green,
and the mitochondria is in red.

544
00:28:49,350 --> 00:28:52,040
And what you see is there
is this mitochondria tubule,

545
00:28:52,040 --> 00:28:55,500
and it's crossed by
the ER right here.

546
00:28:55,500 --> 00:28:58,680
Now, focus on this
when the movie plays.

547
00:28:58,680 --> 00:29:01,680
You're going to see that the
mitochondria undergoes fission

548
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:04,830
right at these points, where
the ER and the mitochondria

549
00:29:04,830 --> 00:29:06,570
intersect.

550
00:29:06,570 --> 00:29:09,660
So that illustrates-- and
now they've mechanistically

551
00:29:09,660 --> 00:29:13,350
dissected what sort of makes
the mitochondria undergo fission

552
00:29:13,350 --> 00:29:15,360
at these crossing sites.

553
00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:17,700
But it really
illustrates the real sort

554
00:29:17,700 --> 00:29:20,340
of complexity and
dynamics that are

555
00:29:20,340 --> 00:29:22,770
present in a cell,
which you might not

556
00:29:22,770 --> 00:29:25,170
be getting from your book.

557
00:29:25,170 --> 00:29:28,020
Oh, I did want to mention that--

558
00:29:28,020 --> 00:29:30,330
so there's a chapter
in your book--

559
00:29:30,330 --> 00:29:33,030
I think it's Key Concept 5.3--

560
00:29:33,030 --> 00:29:36,090
where you can read about
all the organelles.

561
00:29:36,090 --> 00:29:40,560
You should read that and know
roughly what the organelles do.

562
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:42,420
I mean, I could
lecture about that,

563
00:29:42,420 --> 00:29:46,470
but it would just be so
boring that I can't do it.

564
00:29:46,470 --> 00:29:49,290
I'd have to do a
ton of push-ups.

565
00:29:49,290 --> 00:29:55,150
So we have to sort of choose
what we lecture about.

566
00:29:55,150 --> 00:29:58,170
So I would just suggest you
read that part in the textbook.

567
00:29:58,170 --> 00:30:01,500
If you have questions,
come talk to me.

568
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:04,370
And just be familiar with what
your organelles are doing.

569
00:30:04,370 --> 00:30:04,888
Yes, Ory?

570
00:30:04,888 --> 00:30:06,180
AUDIENCE: What was the chapter?

571
00:30:06,180 --> 00:30:08,535
ADAM MARTIN: It's 5.3.

572
00:30:08,535 --> 00:30:09,900
AUDIENCE: Is it like a chapter?

573
00:30:09,900 --> 00:30:12,210
ADAM MARTIN: It's
Key Concept 5.3.

574
00:30:12,210 --> 00:30:14,453
It's probably listed in
the assignments, right?

575
00:30:14,453 --> 00:30:16,120
AUDIENCE: It's a
section in the chapter.

576
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:16,870
ADAM MARTIN: Yeah.

577
00:30:16,870 --> 00:30:18,837
AUDIENCE: It's
only a little bit.

578
00:30:18,837 --> 00:30:20,298
It's not going to be long.

579
00:30:20,298 --> 00:30:24,690


580
00:30:24,690 --> 00:30:25,810
ADAM MARTIN: All right.

581
00:30:25,810 --> 00:30:29,080
I have one last part,
which is to prepare you

582
00:30:29,080 --> 00:30:31,210
for Friday's lecture.

583
00:30:31,210 --> 00:30:32,920
In Friday's lecture,
we're going to start

584
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:34,750
talking about genetics.

585
00:30:34,750 --> 00:30:36,370
And before we talk
about genetics,

586
00:30:36,370 --> 00:30:38,740
we're going to talk
about something that is--

587
00:30:38,740 --> 00:30:40,870
we're going to lay the
groundwork, essentially,

588
00:30:40,870 --> 00:30:44,050
for genetics by talking
about how cells divide.

589
00:30:44,050 --> 00:30:46,750
So I think one of the
most miraculous things

590
00:30:46,750 --> 00:30:51,400
that cells do is that they
can undergo this trick where

591
00:30:51,400 --> 00:30:54,640
they replicate themselves and
split it into two daughter

592
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:55,250
cells.

593
00:30:55,250 --> 00:30:58,090
And you're seeing here, there
are chromosomes in the middle

594
00:30:58,090 --> 00:30:58,870
here.

595
00:30:58,870 --> 00:31:02,030
They're going to line up
along the metaphase plate.

596
00:31:02,030 --> 00:31:04,790
And now they're going to get
pulled to separate sides.

597
00:31:04,790 --> 00:31:07,030
They're going to wiggle
back and forth first.

598
00:31:07,030 --> 00:31:09,740


599
00:31:09,740 --> 00:31:12,350
Then, in just a minute, they're
going to get segregated.

600
00:31:12,350 --> 00:31:13,761
They'll go eventually.

601
00:31:13,761 --> 00:31:17,530


602
00:31:17,530 --> 00:31:19,690
There they go.

603
00:31:19,690 --> 00:31:21,550
So you see-- and
then the cell is

604
00:31:21,550 --> 00:31:25,360
going to pinch at the equator
and divide in two, OK?

605
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:27,850
So now I'm at this last
part, the cytoskeleton,

606
00:31:27,850 --> 00:31:32,260
because the cytoskeleton
is the answer to part

607
00:31:32,260 --> 00:31:36,490
of how these cells divide.

608
00:31:36,490 --> 00:31:39,580
And before I present
the cytoskeleton,

609
00:31:39,580 --> 00:31:42,070
I just want to briefly
mention chromosomes

610
00:31:42,070 --> 00:31:43,730
and what they look like.

611
00:31:43,730 --> 00:31:46,360
So the chromosomes
are your nuclear DNA.

612
00:31:46,360 --> 00:31:49,600
They're linear pieces of DNA,
as opposed to the mitochondria,

613
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:51,830
which has circular DNA.

614
00:31:51,830 --> 00:31:54,700
And again, the fact that
mitochondria have circular DNA

615
00:31:54,700 --> 00:31:58,630
is analogous with sort of
bacterial chromosomes, where

616
00:31:58,630 --> 00:32:02,350
bacteria have circular DNA.

617
00:32:02,350 --> 00:32:06,190
But our chromosomes are
linear pieces of DNA.

618
00:32:06,190 --> 00:32:09,160
And you guys have probably
all seen chromosome spreads

619
00:32:09,160 --> 00:32:12,620
that look like this.

620
00:32:12,620 --> 00:32:17,440
So this would be a
chromosome that's replicated.

621
00:32:17,440 --> 00:32:19,870
There are two
copies, one and two.

622
00:32:19,870 --> 00:32:27,310
So this is a replicated and
condensed chromosome, right?

623
00:32:27,310 --> 00:32:30,010
Initially, chromosomes are
just like bowls of spaghetti.

624
00:32:30,010 --> 00:32:31,690
Everything's mixed together.

625
00:32:31,690 --> 00:32:35,290
But during mitosis, the
chromosomes condense, and then

626
00:32:35,290 --> 00:32:38,260
they sort of resolve
from each other, such

627
00:32:38,260 --> 00:32:40,840
that you can see the
arms of the chromosome.

628
00:32:40,840 --> 00:32:43,630
And you can see where the
chromosomes are coupled

629
00:32:43,630 --> 00:32:45,980
to each other at
this structure here,

630
00:32:45,980 --> 00:32:47,867
which is known as
the centromere.

631
00:32:47,867 --> 00:32:52,180


632
00:32:52,180 --> 00:32:55,870
And at this centromere, a
protein complex assembles.

633
00:32:55,870 --> 00:32:59,380


634
00:32:59,380 --> 00:33:01,720
I'll just draw it like this.

635
00:33:01,720 --> 00:33:04,489
This protein complex is
called the kinetochore.

636
00:33:04,489 --> 00:33:08,710


637
00:33:08,710 --> 00:33:11,140
The kinetochore is a
complex of hundreds

638
00:33:11,140 --> 00:33:15,730
of proteins that assemble
into this large platform that

639
00:33:15,730 --> 00:33:17,800
sits on the centromere.

640
00:33:17,800 --> 00:33:20,650
And the kinetochore
is able to attach

641
00:33:20,650 --> 00:33:24,880
to the cell's cytoskeleton,
specifically microtubules.

642
00:33:24,880 --> 00:33:30,400
So this attaches
to microtubules.

643
00:33:30,400 --> 00:33:34,300
And I will tell you what
microtubules are right now.

644
00:33:34,300 --> 00:33:39,220
So microtubules are a component
of the cell's cytoskeleton.

645
00:33:39,220 --> 00:33:44,600


646
00:33:44,600 --> 00:33:48,970
So the cell's cytoskeleton is
a network of filamentous rods

647
00:33:48,970 --> 00:33:50,950
that are present in the cell.

648
00:33:50,950 --> 00:33:52,870
And the term
cytoskeleton kind of

649
00:33:52,870 --> 00:33:55,360
makes it sound boring,
I think, because it

650
00:33:55,360 --> 00:33:56,890
makes it sound static.

651
00:33:56,890 --> 00:34:00,100
But the cytoskeleton
is anything but static.

652
00:34:00,100 --> 00:34:03,500
It's actually a dynamic
machine in the cell.

653
00:34:03,500 --> 00:34:07,210
And these machines that are
assembled from these fibers

654
00:34:07,210 --> 00:34:08,514
are able to generate force.

655
00:34:08,514 --> 00:34:11,170


656
00:34:11,170 --> 00:34:14,889
So these microtubules
and the cytoskeleton,

657
00:34:14,889 --> 00:34:18,400
they generate force.

658
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:23,050
You can think of them
as motors or machines.

659
00:34:23,050 --> 00:34:24,954
So there are machines
that generate force.

660
00:34:24,954 --> 00:34:27,469


661
00:34:27,469 --> 00:34:31,000
And I'll just illustrate
this with a couple of videos

662
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,170
and slides.

663
00:34:33,170 --> 00:34:38,260
So, again, this is not a stable
structure, but very dynamic.

664
00:34:38,260 --> 00:34:41,710
So this is going to be a
movie where, in green here,

665
00:34:41,710 --> 00:34:46,280
microtubules are labeled, and
the red label's the nucleus.

666
00:34:46,280 --> 00:34:49,389
And these cells
in this fly embryo

667
00:34:49,389 --> 00:34:53,332
are going to undergo
cycles of nuclear division.

668
00:34:53,332 --> 00:34:55,540
And so you're going to see
the microtubules assemble,

669
00:34:55,540 --> 00:34:59,080
disassemble,
assemble, disassemble.

670
00:34:59,080 --> 00:35:01,870
So you see how dynamic
this process is.

671
00:35:01,870 --> 00:35:04,720
The cell is able to assemble
this force-generating

672
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:08,470
apparatus, which is known
as the mitotic spindle, each

673
00:35:08,470 --> 00:35:10,390
and every cell division.

674
00:35:10,390 --> 00:35:12,910
So this structure,
or machine, is

675
00:35:12,910 --> 00:35:16,180
critical to physically
segregate the chromosomes

676
00:35:16,180 --> 00:35:17,920
to opposite poles of a cell.

677
00:35:17,920 --> 00:35:22,180


678
00:35:22,180 --> 00:35:24,640
So now I'm going to tell
you about the microtubules

679
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:27,790
themselves.

680
00:35:27,790 --> 00:35:36,050
So microtubules, as
the name implies,

681
00:35:36,050 --> 00:35:39,250
are sort of tube-like polymers.

682
00:35:39,250 --> 00:35:45,340
So these microtubules
are biopolymers,

683
00:35:45,340 --> 00:35:49,480
which means that cells are
expressing sort of genes that

684
00:35:49,480 --> 00:35:53,140
encode for proteins that
form a subunit that can then

685
00:35:53,140 --> 00:35:57,440
self-assemble into a
larger rod-like structure.

686
00:35:57,440 --> 00:36:01,070
And you can see one of these
rod-like structures here.

687
00:36:01,070 --> 00:36:03,580
They essentially
look like straws.

688
00:36:03,580 --> 00:36:07,510
They're about 25
nanometers in diameter.

689
00:36:07,510 --> 00:36:09,940
And I'm going to show
you this video showing

690
00:36:09,940 --> 00:36:12,520
you microtubules both
disassembling at first

691
00:36:12,520 --> 00:36:13,900
and then assembling.

692
00:36:13,900 --> 00:36:16,330
So that's a dissembling
microtubule.

693
00:36:16,330 --> 00:36:19,420
But they also assemble
to form these longer

694
00:36:19,420 --> 00:36:23,200
rod-like structures.

695
00:36:23,200 --> 00:36:29,310
So these biopolymers
are dynamic,

696
00:36:29,310 --> 00:36:34,540
and they both assemble
but also disassemble.

697
00:36:34,540 --> 00:36:40,430
And both the assembly and the
disassembly can generate force.

698
00:36:40,430 --> 00:36:43,900
Microtubules can push if they
polymerize into something.

699
00:36:43,900 --> 00:36:45,010
They push it, right?

700
00:36:45,010 --> 00:36:50,030
Just like you're kind of poking
someone with your finger.

701
00:36:50,030 --> 00:36:52,660
Now, they also
disassemble, and when

702
00:36:52,660 --> 00:36:56,690
they shrink and disassemble,
they can actually pull.

703
00:36:56,690 --> 00:37:00,590
And I'll show you an
example of that right here.

704
00:37:00,590 --> 00:37:04,030
So this is an example that's
reconstituted, meaning they're

705
00:37:04,030 --> 00:37:05,590
just purified proteins here.

706
00:37:05,590 --> 00:37:07,830
There's no cell.

707
00:37:07,830 --> 00:37:10,720
And what this is, is a bead.

708
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:14,890
And you see the dark stripe
here is a microtubule.

709
00:37:14,890 --> 00:37:16,010
You can see it growing.

710
00:37:16,010 --> 00:37:17,770
There's the end right there.

711
00:37:17,770 --> 00:37:20,510
That microtubule's
going to grow.

712
00:37:20,510 --> 00:37:22,670
It's going to grow
out to about here.

713
00:37:22,670 --> 00:37:24,638
And then at the
end of the movie,

714
00:37:24,638 --> 00:37:26,680
it's going to stop growing,
and the microtubule's

715
00:37:26,680 --> 00:37:27,755
going to shrink back.

716
00:37:27,755 --> 00:37:30,130
And you're going to see that
when the microtubule shrinks

717
00:37:30,130 --> 00:37:33,430
back, it's going to actually
pull this large bead

718
00:37:33,430 --> 00:37:37,518
and pull it towards the
left side of the slide.

719
00:37:37,518 --> 00:37:38,060
Here it goes.

720
00:37:38,060 --> 00:37:39,670
It depolymerizes and pulls.

721
00:37:39,670 --> 00:37:41,170
You see it?

722
00:37:41,170 --> 00:37:44,170
So these microtubules
can generate

723
00:37:44,170 --> 00:37:48,400
a pulling force that's strong
enough to pull this glass bead.

724
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:54,070
And also, it's strong enough
to pull an entire chromosome.

725
00:37:54,070 --> 00:37:56,160
It's actually much stronger--

726
00:37:56,160 --> 00:37:58,180
it generates much
more force than it

727
00:37:58,180 --> 00:38:01,030
requires to pull a chromosome.

728
00:38:01,030 --> 00:38:04,060
So it seems like there's
some robustness in the system

729
00:38:04,060 --> 00:38:05,680
so that it's generating
a pull that's

730
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:08,800
much stronger than needed to
actually drag a chromosome

731
00:38:08,800 --> 00:38:12,370
through viscus media.

732
00:38:12,370 --> 00:38:15,550
So during mitosis, the
way this system is set up

733
00:38:15,550 --> 00:38:18,940
is there's what's known
as a bipolar spindle.

734
00:38:18,940 --> 00:38:20,140
I'll just write down that.

735
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:23,260
We'll come back to it
in Friday's lecture.

736
00:38:23,260 --> 00:38:24,595
There's a bipolar spindle.

737
00:38:24,595 --> 00:38:28,620


738
00:38:28,620 --> 00:38:34,810
And the bipolar spindle is made
up of a number of microtubules.

739
00:38:34,810 --> 00:38:39,550
So here, you can see there
are two poles, here and here.

740
00:38:39,550 --> 00:38:43,300
And in the middle, along the
sort of equator of the cell,

741
00:38:43,300 --> 00:38:44,980
the chromosomes will line up.

742
00:38:44,980 --> 00:38:47,230
There are just two
chromosomes here.

743
00:38:47,230 --> 00:38:50,770
And you can see how microtubules
reach out from the pole

744
00:38:50,770 --> 00:38:54,190
and attach to both sides
of that chromosome.

745
00:38:54,190 --> 00:38:58,390
And you can imagine that
when these microtubules are

746
00:38:58,390 --> 00:39:01,240
told to shrink and
disassemble, if they're

747
00:39:01,240 --> 00:39:04,955
able to remain attached to that
kinetochore, which they are,

748
00:39:04,955 --> 00:39:06,580
when they shrink,
they're going to pull

749
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:11,270
the two copies of the chromosome
away from each other, OK?

750
00:39:11,270 --> 00:39:13,870
And so this is the
basic machinery

751
00:39:13,870 --> 00:39:17,650
that allows for chromosomes
to be segregated in cells.

752
00:39:17,650 --> 00:39:20,340


753
00:39:20,340 --> 00:39:22,700
OK, that's it for today.

754
00:39:22,700 --> 00:39:25,340
Any questions?

755
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:26,300
All right, terrific.

756
00:39:26,300 --> 00:39:28,130
Good luck on your
exam on Wednesday.

757
00:39:28,130 --> 00:39:29,980
It's in here.

758
00:39:29,980 --> 00:40:00,000